"""Days 5–6: a real release procedure and ongoing operations."""
L(4,'1. Assemble a release before touching public traffic','Prepare an identifiable, tested artifact and a reversible release record.',
 [('Release manifest','A small record connecting source revision, immutable image identity, configuration version, schema expectation and verification status.'),('Staging and promotion','Staging rehearses the release with fictional/test data. Promotion uses the reviewed artifact rather than rebuilding an unidentified different one at the last moment.')],
 'Begin a release with evidence: current Python tests, actual image build, real PostgreSQL integration, known migration head and a restore rehearsal. Then record what was tested. A human-readable tag helps navigation, while the selected digest and source revision make the release traceable.\n\nKeep environment-specific credentials out of the manifest. It may name a secret configuration version but must not contain secret values. Define the previous compatible artifact before switching traffic. This course does not create a public hosting account for you; its project and checklist prepare an owned staging deployment. Public traffic should only move after the selected host’s configuration, cost and recovery requirements are understood.',
 'import json\nmanifest={"source":"example-revision-42","image":"registry.example/notes@sha256:"+"b"*64,"schema":"w42_003","config_version":"staging-1","checks":{"python":True,"containers":False}}\nprint(json.dumps({"schema":manifest["schema"],"all_checks":all(manifest["checks"].values())},indent=2))',
 'Add a previous compatible release to a fictional manifest without storing a credential.',
 'release={"candidate":"notes:release-b","previous":"notes:release-a","schema_compatible":True}\nprint("Rollback candidate:",release["previous"])\nprint("Compatibility checked:",release["schema_compatible"])',
 'Reject a release gate while any required verification remains false.',
 'checks={"tests":True,"image":True,"restore":False,"public_smoke":False}\nprint("Release ready:",all(checks.values()))\nprint("Pending:",[name for name,passed in checks.items() if not passed])',
 [('Should a release manifest contain signing-secret values?','No','Yes','Only in staging','Record configuration identity without credentials.'),('Should promotion silently rebuild an unidentified different image?','No','Always','Only if the tag is short','Promote the artifact whose evidence you reviewed.')],
 'Deploy whichever image currently has the latest tag, without a record.','Record the selected source/artifact and its verification before promotion.','Traceability connects a running service to evidence and a recovery option.')

L(4,'2. Configure a Docker web service on a real host','Translate the local recipe into explicit provider settings.',
 [('Provider port contract','A web host usually supplies or expects a listening port. This server accepts PORT before API_PORT and binds to all container interfaces when configured by the image.'),('Application factory','server:create_server builds the app from validated runtime settings. It does not read a real environment when the module is merely imported for an isolated test.')],
 'For the supplied managed-service walkthrough, create a Docker web service from your repository and choose docs/python-week42 as its root. Use that folder’s Dockerfile. Provision PostgreSQL in an appropriate region, configure a restricted runtime URL and a separate migration identity, and put signing material in the provider’s private secret settings.\n\nSet the readiness path to /health/ready where supported, verify PORT and startup behaviour, and review the selected plan’s migration/pre-deploy job support. The local Compose secret paths do not exist automatically on the provider. Use a supported mounted secret file or private environment value; do not copy a local /run/secrets path without configuring its source. The README gives the ordered checklist and manual evidence to capture.',
 'from orm_api.settings import Settings\nsettings=Settings.from_env({"APP_ENV":"production","API_JWT_SECRET":"fictional-staging-"*4,"PORT":"10000"})\nprint("Configured port:",settings.port)\nprint("Environment:",settings.environment)\nprint("Secret in representation:","fictional-staging-" in repr(settings))',
 'Check that malformed provider configuration fails instead of starting on an accidental port.',
 'from orm_api.settings import Settings\nfor port in ["0","70000","not-number"]:\n    try:Settings.from_env({"PORT":port})\n    except ValueError:print("Rejected invalid port")',
 'Verify that the supplied server selects an explicit listening interface.',
 'from pathlib import Path\ntext=Path("server.py").read_text()\nprint("Has interface validation:","API_BIND" in text and "0.0.0.0" in text)\nprint("Uses factory:","factory=True" in text.replace(" ",""))',
 [('Do local Compose secret paths automatically exist on a provider?','No','Always','Only on a free plan','Configure an actual provider-supported secret source.'),('Which port wins in this server: PORT or API_PORT?','PORT','API_PORT','Whichever is alphabetically first','The settings loader makes precedence explicit.')],
 'Paste a local secret mount path into a host dashboard without creating the mount.','Configure the host’s real secret source, port and health contract.','A successful local configuration must be translated to the actual platform, not copied blindly.')

L(4,'3. Migrate once, check compatibility and keep a rollback path','Separate schema evolution from replacing application processes.',
 [('Expand/contract migration','Add compatible structures first, deploy code that can use them, migrate/backfill data carefully, and remove old structures only after old consumers no longer depend on them.'),('Code rollback versus data/schema rollback','Starting a previous image may be safe only if the current schema remains compatible. A destructive database downgrade is a separate operation and may lose information.')],
 'Run one controlled migration job with the migration role before enabling the new web release. The API checks the expected head on startup; it does not try to repair unknown schema history or upgrade independently in every worker. A failed migration is a stop signal, not a reason to reset the database.\n\nFor a real existing database, review generated SQL and data effects on a restored staging copy, take a useful recovery point and test old/new compatibility. The lab’s fresh Week42 lineage is deliberately separate from Week41. The local downgrade command requires explicit permission for destructive work on an owned test/backup copy. Do not confuse a previous code image with a time machine for data.',
 'from orm_api.database import HEAD,REVISIONS\nprint("Expected head:",HEAD)\nprint("Known revisions:",sorted(REVISIONS))\nfrom pathlib import Path\nprint("Explicit destructive guard:","allow_destructive" in Path("manage.py").read_text())',
 'Write an ordered expand/contract plan for adding a new optional field.',
 'steps=["add compatible nullable field","deploy compatible readers/writers","backfill with validation","retire old consumers","review removal separately"]\nfor i,step in enumerate(steps,1):print(i,step)',
 'Decide whether a fictional code rollback passes a schema compatibility gate.',
 'old_supported={"w42_002","w42_003"};current="w42_003"\nprint("Old code compatible:",current in old_supported)\nprint("Rollback deletes new data:",False)',
 [('Does an old image automatically restore old data?','No','Always','Only with a registry','Database state has its own lifecycle and recovery process.'),('What should happen after an unfamiliar migration history is found?','Stop and investigate without resetting data','Delete all tables','Start more workers','Preserve evidence and use a reviewed recovery/migration plan.')],
 'Run a destructive downgrade automatically whenever a new image fails.','Choose a compatible code rollback or a reviewed data recovery plan.','Rollback is a planned compatibility decision, not automatic data destruction.',shell='docker compose run --rm migrate\ndocker compose ps\n# On a provider, run ONE controlled migration job before routing the release.')

L(4,'4. Domain, DNS, HTTPS and the reverse-proxy boundary','Trace a public URL to the correct application without treating DNS as a port map.',
 [('DNS records','A/AAAA identify IP addresses and CNAME aliases a hostname. DNS does not start your server or encode arbitrary HTTP port forwarding.'),('TLS termination and forwarded headers','A trusted edge can terminate HTTPS and forward to the API. Only known proxies should be allowed to supply trusted client/protocol metadata.')],
 'Start with the provider’s HTTPS URL, then add a custom domain if you need one. Configure exactly the record requested by that platform, wait for DNS/certificate provisioning and verify the resulting hostname. A name resolving successfully does not prove the certificate, routing, app readiness or authentication works.\n\nThe supplied server trusts forwarded headers only from loopback by default. A real platform proxy may use another address; review and explicitly configure the actual trust boundary before relying on forwarded protocol/client information. Do not trust arbitrary incoming proxy headers as a shortcut. Credentials sent to an HTTP origin are not protected by a separate HTTPS website that happens to link to it.',
 'from urllib.parse import urlparse\nu=urlparse("https://notes.example.test/health/ready")\nprint("Scheme:",u.scheme)\nprint("Hostname:",u.hostname)\nprint("Path:",u.path)\nprint("Explicit custom port:",u.port)',
 'Separate fictional DNS configuration from application path routing.',
 'dns={"type":"CNAME","name":"notes","target":"service.example.test"}\nprint("DNS target:",dns["target"])\nprint("Contains API path:","/v1/notes" in dns["target"])',
 'Inspect the supplied proxy-trust default rather than assuming all proxies are trusted.',
 'from pathlib import Path\ntext=Path("server.py").read_text().replace(" ","")\nprint("Loopback proxy trust:",\'forwarded_allow_ips="127.0.0.1"\' in text)\nprint("Wildcard proxy trust:",\'forwarded_allow_ips="*"\' in text)',
 [('Does a DNS record start Uvicorn?','No','Yes','Only with a custom domain','DNS naming, routing and process lifecycle are separate.'),('Should every client be allowed to forge trusted forwarded headers?','No','Yes','Only with HTTPS','Trust only the intended reverse-proxy boundary.')],
 'Trust every forwarded header and assume DNS proves HTTPS readiness.','Verify certificate, routing and the intended proxy trust explicitly.','Public transport and proxy metadata each need a clear, testable boundary.')

L(4,'5. Smoke-test the deployed user flow, including rejection','Verify that a reachable service still enforces its API contract.',
 [('Deployment smoke test','A small end-to-end check after release: health, readiness, unauthenticated rejection and an authorised fictional create/read flow.'),('External-device verification','Testing the real HTTPS hostname from another network/device checks more than a localhost request. Use fictional staging data and record status without sharing credentials.')],
 'Open the provider URL from your phone using the deployed HTTPS hostname. Check readiness, then use a fictional staging account to create and retrieve a note. Also verify an anonymous request is rejected and another user cannot access the note. A 200 at /health alone does not prove any of those promises.\n\nThe local example rehearses the API flow with TestClient. The supplied container smoke script performs real HTTP against the owned Compose stack. Your public-host smoke remains a separate manual release gate because this course has not created a paid host or published your API. Never paste bearer tokens into lesson notes, URLs, screenshots or shared logs.',
 'from lesson_support import practice,register,login,PRACTICE_PASSWORD\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,c,h):\n    print("Register:",register(c,"releaselearner").status_code)\n    headers=login(c,"releaselearner")\n    r=c.post("/v1/notes",json={"title":"Staging check","body":"Fictional data"},headers=headers)\n    print("Create:",r.status_code)\n    print("Anonymous read:",c.get("/v1/notes").status_code)\n    print("Owned count:",c.get("/v1/notes",headers=headers).json()["total"])',
 'Check the current-user boundary with the pre-seeded fictional account.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,headers):\n    print("Current user:",c.get("/v1/me",headers=headers).status_code)\n    print("Missing login:",c.get("/v1/me").status_code)',
 'Create a credential-free release-evidence record with an explicit pending external check.',
 'evidence={"local_contract":True,"container_http":True,"phone_https":False}\nprint("Public release verified:",all(evidence.values()))\nprint("Pending:",[k for k,v in evidence.items() if not v])',
 [('Does a health 200 prove authorisation works?','No','Yes','Only on a phone','Test the private user flow and rejection paths.'),('Where should bearer credentials be recorded as release evidence?','Nowhere in public notes/screenshots','In the public URL','In a committed manifest','Capture redacted outcomes rather than credential values.')],
 'Call a release complete after loading only its home page.','Verify health, readiness, authorised behaviour and expected rejection from the intended public route.','A reachable process is one part of a working, correctly protected user flow.')

L(4,'6. Rehearse failure and a controlled release rollback','Use a failed gate to choose a recovery action without improvising on real data.',
 [('Release gate and recovery trigger','Predetermined checks decide whether to route traffic, stop promotion or recover. The gate is based on observed behaviour, not optimism.'),('Compatible previous release','A rollback candidate must be available, identifiable and compatible with current schema/configuration. Reverting code does not undo user actions already committed.')],
 'Before promotion, write what failure looks like: readiness fails, authorisation breaks, data checks mismatch, or latency exceeds an agreed bound. Decide who can stop promotion and which previous artifact is compatible. Keep database recovery separate from application routing.\n\nA release that starts but cannot read its database should not receive normal traffic. Preserve logs with request IDs and investigate rather than repeatedly deleting containers and volumes. The owned integration test stops PostgreSQL to prove readiness changes and starts it again to prove recovery. On a public service, rehearse such experiments in staging instead of casually interrupting real users.',
 'observed={"ready":True,"auth":False,"data":True}\ncompatible_previous=True\nprint("Promote:",all(observed.values()))\nprint("Stop and consider compatible rollback:",not all(observed.values()) and compatible_previous)',
 'List the failed gates from a fictional release rehearsal.',
 'checks={"ready":False,"auth":True,"restore":True,"latency":False}\nprint("Failed gates:",[name for name,ok in checks.items() if not ok])',
 'Preserve a previous release reference while changing only the candidate.',
 'plan={"previous":"image-a","candidate":"image-b","data_recovery":"separate reviewed plan"}\nfailed=plan["candidate"];plan["candidate"]=plan["previous"]\nprint("Rejected:",failed)\nprint("Selected compatible candidate:",plan["candidate"])',
 [('Should a failed release gate be ignored because the image built?','No','Always','Only before a deadline','Build success does not prove live behaviour.'),('Does code rollback undo committed user data?','No','Yes','Only with containers','Choose a separate data recovery process when needed.')],
 'Keep promoting despite failed authorisation/readiness checks.','Stop the release and use the documented compatible recovery path.','Failure evidence is useful only if it can change the release decision.')

L(5,'1. HTTPS is one layer of operating a public application','Separate transport, authentication, proxy trust and abuse controls.',
 [('HTTPS certificate and renewal','TLS protects transport to the verified hostname. Certificates must be valid and renew successfully; it does not repair broken authorisation.'),('Public-service readiness','Beyond a working demo: request limits, login abuse protection, secret rotation, recovery, dependency maintenance and operational ownership must fit the service’s actual users.')],
 'A public URL should use HTTPS, and HTTP should follow a deliberate redirect/challenge policy. Verify the certificate’s hostname, expiration and renewal path. If an edge terminates TLS, keep its upstream routing and trusted proxy settings explicit.\n\nThe Notes API validates input, hashes practice passwords, checks note ownership and uses a restricted database identity. It is still an educational service: the supplied lab is not a complete public account platform with password recovery, a hardened edge and a production abuse-control policy. Use fictional staging data while learning and close those concrete gaps before accepting real personal data. HTTPS, authentication and least privilege remain separate checks.',
 'controls={"HTTPS":"transport","bearer validation":"authentication","note owner filter":"authorisation","runtime DB role":"least privilege","request limit":"resource/abuse control"}\nfor control,purpose in controls.items():print(control+":",purpose)',
 'Show why HTTPS alone does not satisfy all fictional release controls.',
 'controls={"https":True,"authorisation":False,"restore_test":False}\nprint("All requirements met:",all(controls.values()))',
 'Inspect the API’s anonymous private-resource rejection again after transport planning.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,c,h):\n    response=c.get("/v1/notes")\n    print("Status:",response.status_code)\n    print("Bearer challenge:",response.headers.get("WWW-Authenticate"))',
 [('Does HTTPS fix a missing owner filter?','No','Yes','Only in production mode','Transport protection and authorisation are distinct.'),('Is a working staging demo automatically a complete public account service?','No','Yes','Only after a green build','Close the real service’s operational and abuse-control gaps.')],
 'Treat an HTTPS padlock as proof that every application control is correct.','Verify each transport, identity, access and operational boundary separately.','Security claims should name the specific control and evidence.')

L(5,'2. Back up the database and rehearse a separate restore','Distinguish making a file from proving recoverable application data.',
 [('Logical dump and restore','pg_dump exports a logical PostgreSQL backup. pg_restore can restore a custom-format dump. Restoring into a separate database avoids overwriting the source during a drill.'),('RPO and RTO','Recovery Point Objective describes acceptable data-loss time; Recovery Time Objective describes acceptable recovery duration. Choose objectives that match actual backup and restore evidence.')],
 'A backup command returning successfully is only the first step. Record its time, protect access, retain an independent copy, and restore it into a separate database. Validate schema revision, representative rows and application access. Include roles/grants and required runtime secrets in the broader recovery plan without storing them in public notes.\n\nFor PostgreSQL, use database-aware tools rather than copying live data files casually. The terminal recipe writes a custom dump inside the owned database container and copies it out; this also avoids binary-output redirection surprises in older Windows PowerShell. The Python example uses SQLite’s backup API on a tiny owned fixture to teach restore verification. It is not presented as a PostgreSQL backup.',
 'import sqlite3\nfrom tempfile import TemporaryDirectory\nfrom pathlib import Path\nwith TemporaryDirectory() as d:\n    source=sqlite3.connect(Path(d)/"source.sqlite");restored=sqlite3.connect(Path(d)/"restored.sqlite")\n    source.execute("CREATE TABLE notes(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,title TEXT)");source.execute("INSERT INTO notes VALUES(1,?)",("Fictional backup check",));source.commit()\n    source.backup(restored)\n    print("Restored:",restored.execute("SELECT title FROM notes").fetchone()[0])\n    source.close();restored.close()',
 'Calculate the age of a fictional backup in minutes.',
 'from datetime import datetime,timezone\nbackup=datetime(2026,1,1,10,0,tzinfo=timezone.utc);failure=datetime(2026,1,1,10,45,tzinfo=timezone.utc)\nprint("Potential loss window minutes:",int((failure-backup).total_seconds()/60))',
 'Require both a backup artifact and a verified restore before claiming recovery readiness.',
 'evidence={"dump_created":True,"independent_copy":True,"restore_validated":False}\nprint("Recovery demonstrated:",all(evidence.values()))',
 [('Does a backup file alone prove restore works?','No','Always','Only if large','Rehearse restore and validate useful data.'),('Should the first restore drill overwrite your source database?','No','Always','Only with containers','Use a separate owned restore target.')],
 'Copy live database files blindly and assume the backup is usable.','Use database-aware backup and validate a separate restore.','A recovery claim requires evidence that the right data can actually be used again.',shell='docker compose exec -T db pg_dump -U studyhub_owner -d studyhub -Fc -f /tmp/week42.dump\n# Create an owned backups folder, then copy the binary artifact:\npython -c "from pathlib import Path; Path(\'backups\').mkdir(exist_ok=True)"\ndocker compose cp db:/tmp/week42.dump ./backups/week42.dump\n# The README shows a separate restore target; never overwrite source data for a drill.')

L(5,'3. Monitor useful outcomes and make alerts actionable','Distinguish a local healthcheck from external uptime and incident response.',
 [('External uptime check','A probe outside the app’s own process/host can test its intended public route. It requires a configured schedule, bounded timeout and a real notification destination.'),('Alert threshold and recovery','Use a deliberate failure window to avoid noisy one-off alerts, and define what recovery means. An alert needs an owner and a first diagnostic action.')],
 'Use liveness for process viability, readiness for dependencies and a small external user-flow check for public reachability. Watch error rates, latency, disk/volume capacity, connection pressure and backup age. Keep labels bounded and logs free of request bodies, raw credentials and uncontrolled query strings.\n\nA Docker healthcheck does not automatically send you a notification. Monitoring is not complete because a function is named monitor. The Python exercise evaluates a fixed fictional sequence; it does not create a scheduled service. When you later configure a monitoring provider, choose the route, interval, timeout, thresholds, recipient and incident owner explicitly, then test the alert and recovery path.',
 'observations=[True,False,False,False,True]\nfailures=0;alerted=False\nfor ok in observations:\n    failures=0 if ok else failures+1\n    if failures>=3 and not alerted:print("Alert: three consecutive failures");alerted=True\n    if ok and alerted:print("Recovered");alerted=False',
 'Detect an old fictional backup using a maximum age.',
 'age_minutes=95;maximum_age=60\nprint("Backup age alert:",age_minutes>maximum_age)',
 'Confirm that the app still exposes separate liveness and database readiness routes.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,c,h):\n    print("Live:",c.get("/health").json()["status"])\n    print("Ready:",c.get("/health/ready").json()["status"])',
 [('Does a Docker healthcheck automatically notify a human?','No','Always','Only when unhealthy','Configure an actual notification/response path.'),('Does evaluating a fixed observation list create monitoring?','No','Yes','Only with a loop','It is a local policy exercise, not a scheduler or external probe.')],
 'Call monitoring finished because the health route exists.','Configure and test external checks, thresholds, notifications and ownership.','Observability becomes useful operations when it leads to timely, specific action.')

L(5,'4. Scale processes with a database connection budget','Calculate how replicas and worker pools multiply resource demand.',
 [('Worker and replica','A worker is an application process; a replica is another deployed instance. Each process can own its own pool and memory.'),('Connection budget','Estimate workers × replicas × (pool_size + max_overflow), then reserve capacity for migrations, monitoring and administration. Actual limits also depend on platform/pooler behaviour.')],
 'More processes can help some workloads but can also exhaust the database or memory. The lab starts one Uvicorn worker and uses a bounded SQLAlchemy pool of five connections with no overflow. This makes its initial connection budget explicit. Add replicas only after measuring latency, throughput, contention and database limits.\n\nIn-memory request counters are per process; adding workers does not turn them into a global metric. Local SQLite files and per-container state are not a shared multi-replica data architecture. PostgreSQL supplies the external shared store here, but application correctness still depends on transactions, constraints, idempotency where needed and a compatible migration strategy.',
 'replicas=3;workers=2;pool_size=5;overflow=0\nconnections=replicas*workers*(pool_size+overflow)\nprint("Planning maximum:",connections)\nprint("Plus admin reserve:",connections+5)',
 'Compare one and four fictional replicas under the same per-worker pool.',
 'for replicas in [1,4]:print("replicas",replicas,"connections",replicas*1*5)',
 'Check the actual bounded pool configuration in the project.',
 'from pathlib import Path\ntext=Path("orm_api/database.py").read_text().replace(" ","")\nprint("Pool size five:","pool_size=5" in text)\nprint("No overflow:","max_overflow=0" in text)',
 [('Does each worker necessarily share one in-memory counter automatically?','No','Yes','Only on a VPS','Process-local state needs aggregation for a global view.'),('Can adding replicas exhaust database connections?','Yes','Never','Only with SQLite','Budget every process and its pool, with reserve capacity.')],
 'Increase worker count without considering database connections or memory.','Measure the bottleneck and budget shared resources before scaling.','Scaling is a resource and correctness decision, not just a larger process count.')

L(5,'5. Cost, maintenance and incident ownership','Turn a deployed demo into a service with explicit operating responsibilities.',
 [('Cost guardrail','A budget/alert policy covering the complete service. Some alerts warn after costs accrue; a notification is not automatically a spending cap.'),('Runbook','A short, usable guide for a specific operational event: symptoms, safe first checks, owner, recovery path and verification.')],
 'Include compute, managed database, backup storage, logs, egress and domain renewal in the cost model. Track unexpected traffic and retention growth. Verify whether a provider’s spending control actually stops resources or only notifies you, and understand the data/availability consequences before configuring any automatic shutdown.\n\nMaintenance includes base/dependency rebuilds, secret rotation, certificate renewal, restore drills and reviewing failed releases. Each task needs a realistic owner and cadence appropriate to your service. A runbook should guide an incident calmly: check readiness, identify the exact release, inspect redacted correlated logs, preserve data and use the known compatible recovery path. Avoid turning the first response into a destructive reset.',
 'from decimal import Decimal\nfictional={"compute":Decimal("12"),"database":Decimal("15"),"backup":Decimal("4"),"logs":Decimal("3")}\ntotal=sum(fictional.values());budget=Decimal("40")\nprint("Fictional total:",total)\nprint("Remaining planning room:",budget-total)',
 'Create a brief fictional readiness-failure runbook.',
 'runbook=["check readiness status","check selected release","inspect redacted request IDs","check database availability","choose reviewed recovery","verify authorised flow"]\nprint("First:",runbook[0])\nprint("Last:",runbook[-1])',
 'Identify whether a fictional billing notification is a hard spending stop.',
 'policy={"notify_at":30,"automatically_stop":False}\nprint("Hard spending cap:",policy["automatically_stop"])',
 [('Is a billing alert always a hard cost cap?','No','Always','Only with a database','Check the actual policy and its effects.'),('Should a runbook begin by deleting the only data volume?','No','Yes','Only for a small demo','Preserve state and use a reviewed recovery path.')],
 'Ignore ongoing cost and maintenance after the first successful deploy.','Assign owners, budgets, update/rotation work and tested runbooks.','Operating a service continues after its first green health response.')

L(5,'6. Milestone: a containerised and recoverable Notes API','Demonstrate packaging, PostgreSQL integration and a documented public deployment plan.',
 [('Milestone evidence bundle','The actual source, pinned Python dependencies, Dockerfile/context, Compose stack, migration results, container smoke/restore outcomes and an honest public-host verification record.'),('Completion boundary','Local/container verification and a publicly deployed HTTPS user flow are distinct achievements. Mark which you have actually completed; do not invent a public URL or claim an unrun check passed.')],
 'Keep the complete docs/python-week42 project. First run its isolated Python tests. Then initialise the ignored local secrets, build the image and start the Compose API. Verify registration, login, owned CRUD, rejection paths, non-root UID, restricted database role, recreation persistence, readiness failure/recovery and a dump restored into a separate database. The workflow contains real container checks for those claims.\n\nFor the public part, follow the README’s managed-service checklist with an account and budget you choose. Supply private runtime settings, run one migration job, verify HTTPS and the actual user flow from your phone, then document the selected source/artifact, recovery plan and cost/monitoring ownership. Until those public steps are performed, record them as pending. Study Hub’s own static deployment and all previous course progress remain separate.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nrequired=["Dockerfile",".dockerignore","compose.yaml","server.py","manage.py","healthcheck.py","ops.py","container_smoke.py"]\nprint("Project files present:",all(Path(name).is_file() for name in required))\nprint("Required runtime roles:",["studyhub_runtime","studyhub_migrator"])',
 'Rehearse an authenticated fictional read using the same project contract.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,headers):\n    print("Readiness:",c.get("/health/ready").status_code)\n    print("Authenticated notes:",c.get("/v1/notes",headers=headers).status_code)\n    print("Anonymous notes:",c.get("/v1/notes").status_code)',
 'Write an honest checkpoint record that leaves unperformed public work pending.',
 'milestone={"python_checks":True,"container_evidence_reviewed":False,"public_https_phone_flow":False,"restore_plan_written":True}\nprint("Complete:",all(milestone.values()))\nprint("Next evidence:",[k for k,v in milestone.items() if not v])',
 [('Can public deployment be claimed before testing the actual public hostname?','No','Yes','Only if Compose works','Record each verification boundary honestly.'),('Does this practice API replace Study Hub browser progress?','No','Yes','Only after login','Its fictional notes and the learning platform retain separate stores.')],
 'Publish a screenshot of local health and call every deployment requirement finished.','Keep a truthful evidence record for local, container, database, recovery and public-host checks.','A strong milestone is reproducible, explainable and honest about what remains to be verified.',shell='python -m pytest -q\npython -m ruff check .\npython -m ruff format --check .\npython ops.py init-secrets\ndocker compose up --build -d --wait api\n# Open http://127.0.0.1:8042/docs\n# For a fresh disposable CI stack only: python container_smoke.py')
